Ladybird keeps a canvas’s width and height attributes when it takes a
transferred bitmap, and stretches the bitmap to fill the canvas’s box.
Blink does the same: ImageBitmapRenderingContext::SetImage() leaves the
attributes alone, and HTMLCanvasPainter::PaintReplaced() stretches the
image into the replaced-content rect. WebKit and Gecko instead resize
the canvas to the bitmap, which rewrites its attributes: WebKit’s
ImageBitmapRenderingContext calls setSizeForControllingContext(), and
Gecko’s calls HTMLCanvasElement::SetSize(). All three engines throw
InvalidStateError for a detached bitmap. Blink and WebKit then leave the
output as it was, as Ladybird does, but Gecko has already cleared it by
then, in its TransferFromImageBitmap(). And none of the three applies
alpha:false to the pixels: Blink only marks its compositor layer opaque,
and WebKit and Gecko ignore the setting.
